title: Why records are matched only by position
tags: Sync
kind: rationale
summary: Sync never links two records on a similarity of fields or on equal content: a small difference can carry a different meaning, and a file's place can be what matters — a correspondence you want is given a position, or made by hand.

The linking phase finds a record's counterpart by its position — the path of each
of its `tree_ref` and `hierarchy_ref` fields —, and a record with no position only
by an exact twin ([[Matching records across repositories]]). Two looser matches were
considered, and dropped (user, November 2026): there is no better rule to find.

!! No similarity of fields

A record without a position could be linked to the one most like it on the other
side. But two records that differ a little are not two versions of one record: a
small difference — a date, a tag — can carry a very different meaning, and a link
made on a guess would then sync one over the other. Only equality says nothing is
lost. The cost is a duplicate when a field was edited on one side before the first
sync.

A correspondence you want is given a position: a `tree_ref` or `hierarchy_ref`
field holding the same path on both sides links the two records whatever their
other fields ([[TreeRef forest]], [[Hierarchy references]]). Or <<cmd "mf sync link">> names the pair by hand.

!! No equal content

A file moved on one side before its first sync could be found again by its bytes at
its old place on the other side. But a file's place is often what matters: two
identical configuration files written for two different programs are two different
files, and matching them by content would merge them. So the file is matched by its
new path and synced as a new file; the old one stays. The duplicate is easy to see
afterwards ([[Duplicates]]): delete the copy you do not want in one repository, and
the next sync carries the deletion over.
